        <th>0–30 Days (%)</th>
        <th>31–180 Days (%)</th>
        <th>181–365 Days (%)</th>
        <th>Median Min. Nights</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>"""

for city in city_order:
    city_df = df[df['city'] == city]
    total = len(city_df)
    low = 100 * (city_df['availability_365'] <= 30).sum() / total
    mid = 100 * ((city_df['availability_365'] > 30) & (city_df['availability_365'] <= 180)).sum() / total
    high = 100 * (city_df['availability_365'] > 180).sum() / total
    html += f"""
      <tr>
        <td class="city-name">{city}</td>
        <td>{mean_avail[city]:.0f}</td>
        <td>{low:.1f}%</td>
        <td>{mid:.1f}%</td>
        <td>{high:.1f}%</td>
        <td>{med_min_nights[city]:.0f}</td>
      </tr>"""

html += f"""
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS ═══ -->
<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>

  <ol class="takeaway-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Amsterdam is the most regulated market in the dataset.</strong>
      With 53% of listings available 30 days or fewer and the lowest mean availability (93 days/year),
      Amsterdam's short-term rental cap is clearly reflected in the data. For market entrants, this means
      limited supply but potentially higher per-night yields for licensed operators. The low professional host share
      (6.8%) also suggests less competitive pressure from commercial operators.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bangkok and Barcelona are the most professionalized markets.</strong>
      Over 58% of listings in both cities are managed by hosts with 5+ properties. This concentration creates
      barriers for individual hosts entering the market and suggests regulatory attention may be warranted.
      For investors, it signals that competing requires scale; for policymakers, it raises questions about
      housing stock conversion.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Paris has the highest entire-home share (88%) but relatively low professional host concentration (21%).</strong>
      This suggests a market dominated by individual property owners renting out their complete homes rather
      than commercial operators managing portfolios. The high minimum-night requirement (mean: 44 nights)
      indicates that many Paris listings operate as medium-term rentals, likely as a regulatory adaptation
      to France's strict short-term rental rules.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rome shows the strongest guest demand signals.</strong>
      With the highest average reviews per month (1.77) and the highest mean LTM reviews (14.4), Rome
      stands out as the most actively booked market. Combined with high availability (219 days/year mean)
      and moderate professional host share (31%), it presents as an accessible market with strong demand
      and less entrenched commercial competition.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pricing data gaps are a major analytical limitation.</strong>
      Paris and Sydney—the 2nd and 7th largest markets by listing count—have zero price data, making
      definitive cross-market pricing conclusions impossible. Bangkok's prices appear to be in Thai Baht
      rather than USD, further complicating comparisons. Among cities with reliable price data,
      Amsterdam commands the highest median ($222), while Barcelona and Rome are the most affordable
      (median ~$131).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>London dominates by volume but is middling on most quality metrics.</strong>
      With 16,674 listings (33% of the dataset), London is by far the largest market, but its professional
      host share (32%), review velocity (0.99/month), and price levels (median $135) are all near the
      middle of the pack. Its scale offers opportunity but also implies competitive saturation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Low-availability markets (Amsterdam, Paris) and high-availability markets (Bangkok, Rome)
      represent fundamentally different operating environments.</strong>
      Low availability correlates with regulatory stringency and may produce higher occupancy and per-night
      yields for compliant operators. High availability suggests either lighter regulation or excess supply,
      which may pressure pricing but offers more booking flexibility for guests.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout info">
    <strong>Methodology note.</strong> Professional host share is defined as the percentage of listings
    where <code>calculated_host_listings_count &ge; 5</code>. Review metrics are computed only on listings
    with at least one review. All statistics are derived from the 50,000-row dataset as provided; no values
    have been imputed or fabricated. Bangkok price figures are presented as-is from the dataset despite
    apparent currency discrepancies with the documented USD normalization.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══ FOOTER ═══ -->
<div class="footer">
  <p>
    Global Airbnb Market Comparison Report &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data: Inside Airbnb (scraped 2026-03-29) &nbsp;|&nbsp;
    Generated 2026-05-21 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Licensed under CC BY 4.0
  </p>
</div>

</div>
</body>
</html>"""

# ─── Write Output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
os.makedirs('output', exist_ok=True)
output_path = 'output/final_report.html'
with open(output_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"Report written to {output_path}")
print(f"File size: {os.path.getsize(output_path):,} bytes")

